 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shevuot.25a.13",
   "daf": "25a",
   "sentenceId": 12,
   "he": "כֵּיצַד? אָמַר ״שְׁבוּעָה שֶׁאֶתֵּן לִפְלוֹנִי״ וְשֶׁלֹּא אֶתֵּן״. מַאי ״אֶתֵּן״? אִילֵּימָא צְדָקָה לְעָנִי, מוּשְׁבָּע וְעוֹמֵד מֵהַר סִינַי הוּא – שֶׁנֶּאֱמַר: ״נָתוֹן תִּתֵּן לוֹ״!",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: «How so?» Examples are if one «said:» On my «oath I will give so-and-so» some item, «or:» On my oath «I will not give» it to him. «What» is the case of an oath in which he says: «I will give? If we say» that he takes an oath that he will give «charity to a poor person,» that is not an oath that takes effect, since «he is under oath from Mount Sinai» to give charity, «as it is stated» with regard to a poor person: «“You shall give him”» (Deuteronomy 15:10)."
  }
 ],
